  "report": "The poor yields in L47-L48 N/NW edge (site yields 0-60%) vs. good lots L44-L46 N/NW edge (site yields 87-100%) confirm this is a material/process defect in the lots, not a tester artifact. All sites see the same pattern.\n\nNow I have sufficient data to form my conclusion. Let me summarize:\n\n---\n\nROOT_CAUSE_DIMENSIONS: LOT_ID, TEMP_C, VDD, SPATIAL\n\nROOT_CAUSE_VALUES: LOT_ID=L47,L48; TEMP_C=85; VDD=0.75; SPATIAL=edge N,NW\n\nROOT_CAUSE_STRUCTURE: interaction\n\nROOT_CAUSE_EVIDENCE: measured\n\nFINDINGS\n\n1. Overall yield is 91.6% (315 failed parts of 3750). Lots L47 and L48 show 85.33% and 86.13% yield respectively, vs. 95%+ for L44-L46 (get_yield_summary LOT_ID filter).\n\n2. Primary failing tests are core_fmax (0.764%), pll_lock (0.711%), and vmin_core (0.684%), all performance/voltage marginal tests (get_failures_by_test).\n\n3. At TEMP_C=85\u00b0C, overall fail rate is 0.805% vs. 0.107-0.171% at lower temperatures. At VDD=0.75V, overall fail rate is 0.783% vs. 0.142-0.157% at higher voltages (get_fail_rate_by_condition).\n\n4. Spatial analysis shows N octant (0.917% fail) and NW octant (0.895% fail) much worse than other regions; edge band shows 0.535% vs. centre 0.158% (get_yield_by_region).\n\n5. In L47+L48 only, at TEMP_C=85: fail rate 1.78% vs. 0.156% in L44-L46. At VDD=0.75: fail rate 1.716% in L47+L48 vs. 0.164% in L44-L46 (get_fail_rate_by_condition LOT_ID filters).\n\n6. In L47+L48, octants N and NW show 2.062% and 2.048% fail rates respectively, with edge at 1.059%, vs. L44-L46 with 0.153%, 0.127%, and 0.186% in same regions (get_yield_by_region LOT_ID filters).\n\n7. Combined effect in L47+L48 at N/NW edge: VDD=0.75 shows 9.78% fail rate; TEMP_C=85 shows 9.733% fail rate; both simultaneously shows 28.543% fail rate (get_fail_rate_by_condition with LOT_ID, OCTANT, REGION, and stress parameter filters).\n\n8. At the worst condition (L47+L48, N/NW edge, TEMP_C=85, VDD=0.75): core_fmax fails 76.4% (42/55), vmin_core fails 75.0% (39/52), pll_lock fails 59.7% (37/62) (get_failures_by_test with full filter).\n\n9. Distribution analysis: L47+L48 core_fmax has mean 3.8448 GHz with 2.57\u03c3 margin to lower limit (3.61 GHz), vs. L44-L46 with mean 3.848 GHz and 2.99\u03c3 margin (get_distribution_stats core_fmax).\n\n10. Distribution analysis: L47+L48 vmin_core has mean 0.721V with 2.67\u03c3 margin to upper limit (0.765V), vs. L44-L46 with mean 0.7203V and 2.99\u03c3 margin (get_distribution_stats vmin_core).\n\n11. Spatial pattern is consistent across all wafers in L47+L48 (W01-W05 all show N/NW edge defects), ruling out wafer-specific issues (get_yield_by_region LOT_ID and WAFER_ID filters).\n\n12. All tester sites (1-8) show the same spatial pattern in L47+L48 N/NW edge (0-60% yield vs. 87-100% in good lots), ruling out tester contribution (get_yield_summary SITE_NUM filter).\n\nRULED OUT\n\n- Single test failure: Multiple tests fail systematically (core_fmax, vmin_core, pll_lock); not a single test circuit issue.\n- Tester site effect: All eight sites show identical spatial defect pattern; sites 3, 6, 8 achieve 60%+ yield in L47+L48 N/NW edge.\n- Wafer-specific defect: Pattern consistent across W01-W05 within L47-L48 lots.\n- Temperature or voltage alone: Stress conditions drive failures in L47+L48 only; L44-L46 stable across all conditions.\n\nASSESSMENT\n\nThe data establish a clear three-way interaction between lot, test conditions, and spatial location. Lots L47 and L48 exhibit degraded electrical specifications\u2014reduced maximum operating frequency and elevated minimum operating voltage\u2014concentrated most severely in the N and NW edge regions. This is consistent with a localized process defect (e.g., metal contamination, dopant variation, or thermal process excursion) during fabrication of those lots, affecting a specific die population.\n\nThe 28.543% failure rate at the intersection of worst-case conditions (TEMP_C=85, VDD=0.75) in the affected spatial region is a measured value, not inferred. The three dimensions co-occur to produce failures; L44-L46 show no such pattern, confirming this is lot-dependent.\n\nThe failure mechanism is performance marginality driven by both stress (high temperature, low voltage) and silicon defect concentration in specific wafer regions.\n\nRECOMMENDED NEXT STEP\n\n1. **Immediate:** Quarantine remaining die from L47 and L48, particularly from wafer positions corresponding to the N and NW edge octants. Implement 100% functional test at worst-case conditions (85\u00b0C, 0.75V) for these lots if use is unavoidable, or scrap.\n\n2. **Root cause investigation:** Examine process documentation for L47 and L48 for deviations during metallization, implant, or thermal steps that could create spatial contamination or doping gradients. Compare tool-by-tool wafer maps for dopant uniformity or metal coverage anomalies.\n\n3. **Verification:** Pull control wafers from L44-L46 (known-good) and L47-L48 (failed) for physical FA (SEM, EDX, SIMS) focused on N/NW edge die. Check for metal voids, dopant clustering, or oxide defects.\n\n4. **Process follow-up:** After root cause is identified, implement corrective action and process monitoring for subsequent lots to prevent recurrence.",
